        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the angle of the point (x, y): the direction you would face, standing at the origin, to look at it. The angle is measured from the positive x axis, counter-clockwise, from -180 to 180 degrees.\n\natan of y / x tries to do the same and cannot: (1, 1) and (-1, -1) both have y / x = 1, so atan gives 45 for both, though one is up-right and the other down-left. atan2 is given y and x separately, so it knows which quadrant the point is in, and it copes with x = 0 (straight up or down), where y / x has no answer.\n\nUse it to get a heading from a velocity, the angle of a joystick or a hand around a centre, the direction from one point to another (atan2 of their difference), or the phase of a point going round a circle.\n\nSYNTAX:\natan2 <x: float>\nDefault: x 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\natan2 -1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ny:\nThe vertical part of the point. A number, list, array or tensor - each element is paired with the matching element of x. Every input sends: the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nx (float box, also an inlet):\nThe horizontal part. A single number pairs with every y; a list, array or tensor must have the same shape as y. It does not send by itself: send x first, then y.\n\ndegrees:\nA checkbox property, on by default. Off gives radians (-pi to pi).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nangle:\nThe angle in degrees (or radians), the same shape as y.\n\nZERO:\natan2 of y 0, x 0 is 0: the origin has no direction, and 0 is what it says.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: cos and sin of a steadily turning angle trace a circle; atan2 recovers the angle, which climbs steadily from -180 to 180 and then jumps back - the same direction, just counted from the other side.\n\nBottom: the point x -1, y 1 is up and to the left, 135 degrees. atan of 1 / -1 sees only -1 and answers -45, which is down and to the right.\n\nRELATED:\natan and tan work on one ratio; sin and cos go the other way, from an angle to a point. np.linalg.norm gives the point's distance from the origin, which with atan2 is the whole of the point in polar form.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the angle of the point (x, y): the direction you would face, standing at the origin, to look at it. The angle is measured from the positive x axis, counter-clockwise, from -180 to 180 degrees.\n\natan of y / x tries to do the same and cannot: (1, 1) and (-1, -1) both have y / x = 1, so atan gives 45 for both, though one is up-right and the other down-left. atan2 is given y and x separately, so it knows which quadrant the point is in, and it copes with x = 0 (straight up or down), where y / x has no answer.\n\nUse it to get a heading from a velocity, the angle of a joystick or a hand around a centre, the direction from one point to another (atan2 of their difference), or the phase of a point going round a circle.\n\nSYNTAX:\natan2 <x: float>\nDefault: x 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\natan2 -1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ny:\nThe vertical part of the point. A number, list, array or tensor - each element is paired with the matching element of x. Every input sends: the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nx (float box, also an inlet):\nThe horizontal part. A single number pairs with every y; a list, array or tensor must have the same shape as y. It does not send by itself: send x first, then y.\n\ndegrees:\nA checkbox property, on by default. Off gives radians (-pi to pi).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nangle:\nThe angle in degrees (or radians), the same shape as y.\n\nZERO:\natan2 of y 0, x 0 is 0: the origin has no direction, and 0 is what it says.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: cos and sin of a steadily turning angle trace a circle; atan2 recovers the angle, which climbs steadily from -180 to 180 and then jumps back - the same direction, just counted from the other side.\n\nBottom: the point x -1, y 1 is up and to the left, 135 degrees. atan of 1 / -1 sees only -1 and answers -45, which is down and to the right.\n\nRELATED:\natan and tan work on one ratio; sin and cos go the other way, from an angle to a point. np.linalg.norm gives the point's distance from the origin, which with atan2 is the whole of the point in polar form."
        },
